WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
 Never snap to guides.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.540
We want your mind, we
don't want your software.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.250
If everybody loves
your work, you're

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.240
playing it much too safe.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.520
You need some people
not liking it,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.140
and then you're
probably onto something.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.910
But a big part of
graphic design is

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.450
to get somebody to pick up your
book, to click on your site,

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.110
to walk across the street
and read the fine print.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:25.870
Whoa.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.690
If you've done your job as a
designer, people will do that.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.240
In this class, I'll
be showing my work

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.120
and explaining why it
looks the way it does.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.970
We'll look at color and
shape and texture and type.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:45.160
What appears chaotic or random
is almost never the case.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.200
I'm also going to bring
you a design workshop

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.180
that I give frequently
around the world,

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.220
giving assignments that
you can do at home.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.290
The answer to everything
you're working on

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.960
is within the stuff you're
given from the client.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.580
You're going to
have some reaction,

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.380
and then go with that.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.600
I never set out to break rules.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.580
I don't go to a
studio and say, "OK,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.300
what rules can I break today?"

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.080
"How can I make
this hard to read?"

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.385
No, it's what am
I trying to solve?

00:01:09.385 --> 00:01:10.720
What am I trying to say?

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.450
What am I feeling and
what does that look like?

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.560
There's so much slick, fine,
professional, forgettable

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.270
graphic design out there.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.510
It's even more reason we need
a few rebels to make something

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:23.590
stand out.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.000
Just the form sends a message.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.490
Try not to get too literal.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.830
I want to feel that
as much as we see it.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.980
Trust your eye,
trust your intuition.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.410
Every now and then, something
you weren't expecting

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.240
happens in the process.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.350
You got to just trust your gut
and do what you feel is right,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.080
and that's where the really
great works come from.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.540
I'm David Carson, and
this is my MasterClass.

